// CI golden set — scored against eval/fixtures/, which is committed.
//
// Separate from eval/golden.jsonl on purpose. That set references chunk ids
// from documents under corpus/, which is gitignored so nobody publishes their
// own documents by accident — which also means CI can never resolve those ids.
// This set references a fictional corpus that is safe to publish, so the gate
// can actually run on a fresh checkout.
//
// Kept deliberately cheap. Only the three `generation` items call the model;
// everything else stops after reranking, and the two negatives are refused by
// the confidence gate before any model call happens. Regenerate the ids with
// `./ask-my-docs chunks -dir eval/fixtures` after touching the fixtures.
//
// THE CORPUS IS PART OF THE INSTRUMENT. These 19 items once scored
// recall@10 = 1.000, which read as a pass and was really a broken gauge: the
// fixture corpus was 25 chunks and TOP_K is 10, so retrieval returned 40% of
// everything and recall@10 could not have come out otherwise. On that corpus
// the cross-encoder moved nDCG by 0.048, against 0.164 on the real 300-chunk
// corpus — a gate that cannot see the reranker cannot catch it regressing.
//
// eval/gen_distractors.py fixes that by generating confusable near-misses for
// each item here — same component family, same vocabulary, different specifics
// — taking the corpus to 169 chunks. Volume alone would not have done it;
// random filler is trivially separable and leaves the ranking untouched.
//
// Two consequences worth knowing before editing this file:
//   * The distractors are additive. They never modify the five hand-written
//     fixtures, so every chunk id below still points at the same text.
//   * rerank_lift is embedder-dependent, not a constant. Measured on the same
//     169 chunks: bge-m3 retrieves 0.781 and rerank lifts it 0.168; NVIDIA's
//     nemotron-embed retrieves 0.913 and lifts it 0.036. Both finish at nDCG
//     0.9490. A small lift can mean a strong embedder rather than a weak
//     reranker, so read the two numbers together or neither. The committed
//     baseline is the NVIDIA one, because that is what CI runs.
//
// For reference, the lift this replaced: on the original 25-chunk corpus it
// was 0.000 exactly — reranking changed nothing the gate could see. It is now
// 0.168 under the same embedder used to measure the real 300-chunk corpus,
// where the figure is 0.164.
//
// READ ci-baseline.json IN THAT LIGHT. It is recorded by CI, in CI, and CI
// runs bge-reranker-base rather than the bge-reranker-v2-m3 used everywhere
// else — TEI's CPU image has only an ONNX backend and v2-m3 ships no ONNX
// weights, so the container cannot start. On that model the recorded lift is
// NEGATIVE (-0.103): reranking costs more than it returns, because 50
// candidates are reordered and cut to 10 and a weak cross-encoder drops
// chunks plain fusion had ranked inside the window.
//
// So the committed baseline is a tripwire for regressions in this
// repository's code, measured against itself under one fixed configuration.
// It is not this pipeline's retrieval quality and must never be quoted as
// such. The quality figures live in the README and come from the local stack.

// --- sparse leg: exact identifiers, which pure vector search gets wrong ---
{"id": "ci-code-4412", "query": "ORB-4412", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["error-codes.csv#0"], "stage": "retrieval", "answer_contains": "Reissue the token with an explicit tag list"}
{"id": "ci-code-6203", "query": "What does error ORB-6203 mean?", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["error-codes.csv#1"], "stage": "retrieval", "answer_contains": "Drop the offending label before re-enabling the exporter"}
{"id": "ci-serial-rf417", "query": "RF-00417", "relevant_chunk_ids": ["lab-inventory.txt#0"], "stage": "retrieval", "answer_contains": "Spectrum analyser SA-2100"}

